  "culture": {
    "title": "A city of festivals",
    "md": "Athens kept more festivals than any other Greek city; a hostile pamphlet of the 420s complains that the Athenians celebrate twice as many as anyone else, and roughly a third of the days in the year had some religious observance. A festival (ἑορτή) meant no work, a procession (πομπή) through the streets, a sacrifice at an altar (βωμός), and meat: for most families, festivals were the only days they ate it. The priest poured water and barley over the victim, cut a lock of hair from its forehead, and after the killing the thigh bones wrapped in fat were burned for the god while the meat was roasted and shared out.\n\nOur festival is the **Skira**, held on the 12th of Skirophorion, the last month of the Attic year (late June). The priestess of Athena Polias, the priest of Poseidon-Erechtheus and the priest of Helios walked from the Acropolis under a great white sunshade carried by members of the Eteoboutadai clan, out through the Kerameikos along the Sacred Way to Skiron, a sanctuary of Athena and Demeter near the Kephisos on the road to Eleusis. It was a day when the women of Athens gathered on their own; in Aristophanes' *Assemblywomen* they hatch their plot at the Skira. Two days later came the Dipolieia, with its archaic ox-killing for Zeus on the Acropolis, and a month after that the new year opened with the Panathenaia, the greatest festival of all, which the story reaches in Unit 7.",
    "image": "culture-skira"
  },
